WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.100 --> 00:00:08.080
In 1981, a Parisian schoolboy was busy trying
to do something schoolboys the world over

00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:13.410
have been attempting for countless generations
- impress his mates.

00:00:13.410 --> 00:00:15.960
But this particular kid was more inventive
than most.

00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:21.561
He didn’t claim to have been on holiday
to Area 51, or to be the youngest person in

00:00:21.561 --> 00:00:24.410
history to become a black belt in karate.

00:00:24.410 --> 00:00:29.940
No, he claimed to enjoy breaking into places
he shouldn’t after dark.

00:00:29.940 --> 00:00:34.219
Nothing wrong with that - plenty of kids like
bending the rules when they get the chance.

00:00:34.219 --> 00:00:40.210
But as is often the way with childhood boasts,
the boy let bravado get the better him.

00:00:40.210 --> 00:00:45.170
Because he wasn’t talking about a few surreptitious
cigarettes in the car park of the local leisure

00:00:45.170 --> 00:00:46.170
centre.

00:00:46.170 --> 00:00:52.929
No, he claimed the next place on his list
for a little night-time excursion was the

00:00:52.929 --> 00:00:58.280
Panthéon, which just so happens to be one
of the most famous buildings in all of Paris.

00:00:58.280 --> 00:01:04.190
Built in the 18th century and originally a
church, the neoclassical marvel that is the

00:01:04.190 --> 00:01:06.430
Panthéon is steeped in history.

00:01:06.430 --> 00:01:13.130
It’s home to Léon Foucault’s famous pendulum
with which he completed the first ever experiment

00:01:13.130 --> 00:01:15.310
demonstrating the rotation of the earth.

00:01:15.310 --> 00:01:20.920
And today it’s used as a mausoleum, housing
the remains of some of France’s most celebrated

00:01:20.920 --> 00:01:27.780
citizens including Voltaire, Victor Hugo,
Alexandre Dumas, Louis Braille, and Marie

00:01:27.780 --> 00:01:32.929
Curie, who was born in Poland but became a
French citizen in later life after moving

00:01:32.929 --> 00:01:34.219
to Paris.

00:01:34.219 --> 00:01:39.759
So yeah - much like Ron Burgundy, the Panthéon
is kind of a big deal.

00:01:39.759 --> 00:01:44.909
The boy’s friends were quick to call what
was very obviously a bluff, but he refused

00:01:44.909 --> 00:01:46.179
to back down.

00:01:46.179 --> 00:01:51.380
Which is how he and a small group of friends
ended up sneaking out one night when the rest

00:01:51.380 --> 00:01:57.810
of the city was sleeping to attempt to get
inside one of Paris’ most famous landmarks.

00:01:57.810 --> 00:02:05.289
That should have been where this story ends,
with the kids repelled by locked doors and

00:02:05.289 --> 00:02:07.579
patrolling night-watchmen.

00:02:07.579 --> 00:02:10.140
This was the Panthéon, after all.

00:02:10.140 --> 00:02:16.360
But much to their surprise, getting inside
was a piece of cake.

00:02:16.360 --> 00:02:22.140
Being inside was equally breezy, and the group
spent the night exploring the utterly deserted

00:02:22.140 --> 00:02:28.750
national landmark to their heart’s content
without ever running into even a hint of security.

00:02:28.750 --> 00:02:33.830
By the time they finally made their way blearily
to bed in the early hours of the following

00:02:33.830 --> 00:02:40.290
morning, the same question had crystallised
in each of their minds: where next?

00:02:40.290 --> 00:02:43.720
In most cities their options would have been
severely limited.

00:02:43.720 --> 00:02:50.670
But as you’ll know if you watched my recent
video on the Catacombs - Paris is no ordinary

00:02:50.670 --> 00:02:51.670
city.

00:02:51.670 --> 00:02:57.550
The tunnels that lie hidden beneath its streets
are vast - at least 350 kilometres all told

00:02:57.550 --> 00:03:04.220
- and, to those who know where to look, they
offer secret access to the basements of private

00:03:04.220 --> 00:03:09.340
residences, government buildings, and national
monuments alike.

00:03:09.340 --> 00:03:16.260
In short, the Paris catacombs are an urban
explorer’s pulsating nocturnal emission.

00:03:16.260 --> 00:03:21.870
And, according to legend, it was during an
adventure in the catacombs that our group

00:03:21.870 --> 00:03:28.250
of teenagers stumbled across a narrow tunnel
packed with electrical cables, which they

00:03:28.250 --> 00:03:34.380
followed until eventually finding themselves
in the basement of what turned out to be the

00:03:34.380 --> 00:03:36.230
ministry of communications.

00:03:36.230 --> 00:03:41.900
Again, you’d be forgiven for thinking an
important government building like this would

00:03:41.900 --> 00:03:48.690
have been protected by laser sharks and tripwires
- OK, maybe not that, but at least the odd

00:03:48.690 --> 00:03:52.400
ageing guard snoozing on a stool somewhere.

00:03:52.400 --> 00:03:57.790
But it seems Parisians are awfully relaxed
when it comes to security, because the group

00:03:57.790 --> 00:04:01.910
were able to explore the basement of the building
at their leisure.

00:04:01.910 --> 00:04:07.560
And it was there, locked away in a dusty drawer,
that they were to make a discovery that would

00:04:07.560 --> 00:04:15.220
change the history of Paris’ underworld
forever - a set of maps charting the entirety

00:04:15.220 --> 00:04:16.859
of the catacombs.

00:04:16.859 --> 00:04:21.230
For a group of budding urban explorers, it
was the motherlode.

00:04:21.230 --> 00:04:28.350
People had been exploring the catacombs for
untold years, painstakingly mapping new areas

00:04:28.350 --> 00:04:31.290
and uncovering forgotten corners.

00:04:31.290 --> 00:04:35.790
But nobody alive had managed to build up the
kind of comprehensive overview that was contained

00:04:35.790 --> 00:04:38.090
in those pages.

00:04:38.090 --> 00:04:44.000
Secret entrances to the catacombs that had
been lost for generations, entire tunnel systems

00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:50.490
as yet unexplored, and, of course, underground
access points to many of Paris’ most famous

00:04:50.490 --> 00:04:52.140
landmarks.

00:04:52.140 --> 00:04:58.450
Armed with this sacred knowledge, Les UX,
as the group of friends would come to be known,

00:04:58.450 --> 00:05:03.240
have been causing absolute mayhem in Paris
ever since.

00:05:03.240 --> 00:05:08.540
Fast-forward to today and Les UX, which is
short for ‘the urban experiment’, have

00:05:08.540 --> 00:05:15.190
grown into a fully-fledged underground organisation
in both the literal and the figurative sense.

00:05:15.190 --> 00:05:20.040
Figuratively underground because they operate
under a cloak of extreme secrecy, and literally

00:05:20.040 --> 00:05:24.450
underground because it’s their unique knowledge
of the Catacombs that has allowed them to

00:05:24.450 --> 00:05:30.639
pull off the kinds of stunts usually reserved
for plot lines in Hollywood blockbusters.

00:05:30.639 --> 00:05:34.960
By the way - if the name ‘Les UX’ sounds
familiar, that might be because I briefly

00:05:34.960 --> 00:05:38.860
touched on the organisation in my video about
the Catacombs.

00:05:38.860 --> 00:05:44.070
These were the guys who built an entire cinema
complex deep beneath the streets of Paris

00:05:44.070 --> 00:05:49.070
complete with bar and restaurant, where they
are thought to have held film festivals and

00:05:49.070 --> 00:05:55.280
private screenings for months, maybe even
years before French police eventually stumbled

00:05:55.280 --> 00:05:58.120
across the setup during a training exercise.

00:05:58.120 --> 00:06:03.669
But while building a secret cinema in a cave
system below one of the world’s great cities

00:06:03.669 --> 00:06:08.639
is pretty damn cool, it’s just the tip of
Les UX iceberg.

00:06:08.639 --> 00:06:13.810
So, what would you do if someone gave you
what amounted to a skeleton key granting access

00:06:13.810 --> 00:06:19.290
to some of the most famous buildings in the
world through a series of little-known secret

00:06:19.290 --> 00:06:20.290
entrances?

00:06:20.290 --> 00:06:24.419
This is Paris we’re talking about here - pretty
much everywhere you look there are priceless

00:06:24.419 --> 00:06:30.590
works of art hanging on gallery walls, cellars
stocked with dusty bottles of vintage wine,

00:06:30.590 --> 00:06:33.270
and museums chock full of rare artefacts.

00:06:33.270 --> 00:06:37.800
I don’t know about you, but I’d be robbing
the place blind - you wouldn’t be able to

00:06:37.800 --> 00:06:43.400
move in the Thoughty2 residence for Monets
and half-drunk bottles of Cheval Blanc.

00:06:43.400 --> 00:06:45.770
But not Les UX.

00:06:45.770 --> 00:06:52.171
In fact, over the last few decades, far from
stealing everything in sight, they’ve kind

00:06:52.171 --> 00:06:54.040
of been doing the opposite.

00:06:54.040 --> 00:07:01.150
You see, they’re of the opinion French officials
have - at times - been a little negligent

00:07:01.150 --> 00:07:06.580
in their attitude towards the country’s
rich heritage, focusing tax dollars only on

00:07:06.580 --> 00:07:13.639
the upkeep of big-ticket items like the Louvre
and the Eiffel Tower, while letting less well-known

00:07:13.639 --> 00:07:18.580
sites slowly deteriorate or even fall into
ruin.

00:07:18.580 --> 00:07:24.920
So, instead of pulling off a series of art
heists worthy of the Thomas Crown affair,

00:07:24.920 --> 00:07:32.390
Les UX decided to become the invisible champions
of Paris’ lost heritage, both above ground

00:07:32.390 --> 00:07:33.800
and below.

00:07:33.800 --> 00:07:39.930
They’re responsible for secret restoration
of at least one forgotten metro station, a

00:07:39.930 --> 00:07:46.880
12th century crypt, a 100-year-old French
government bunker, and a World War 2 air raid

00:07:46.880 --> 00:07:47.880
shelter.

00:07:47.880 --> 00:07:54.400
Of course, that kind of work takes both manpower
and expertise, and Les UX has grown considerably

00:07:54.400 --> 00:07:59.210
since those early days when it were just a
group of friends having a bit of fun.

00:07:59.210 --> 00:08:04.650
Thought to be made up of at least 150 members
today, the group is now split across several

00:08:04.650 --> 00:08:10.449
different units, each with its own special
skills and equally special name.

00:08:10.449 --> 00:08:17.140
There’s the Mouse House, who focus on infiltration,
the Untergunther, who carry out covert restorations

00:08:17.140 --> 00:08:23.060
of heritage sites around the city, and La
Mexicaine De Perforation, who were responsible

00:08:23.060 --> 00:08:27.910
for the secret cinema complex as well as countless
other excavations in the catacombs.

00:08:27.910 --> 00:08:34.579
But while the names are playful, Les UX are
deadly serious in their mission of stepping

00:08:34.579 --> 00:08:40.379
in where the state is failing to take care
of Paris’ rich history, as well as reclaiming

00:08:40.379 --> 00:08:47.480
lost and forgotten spaces for creative expression,
usually in the catacombs themselves, where

00:08:47.480 --> 00:08:52.660
they regularly host secret art exhibitions
and film screenings.

00:08:52.660 --> 00:08:59.420
In 2006, a lone thief managed to waltz inside
the Parisian Museum of Modern art, helping

00:08:59.420 --> 00:09:05.930
himself to paintings by the likes of Picasso,
Modigliani, and Matisse before escaping entirely

00:09:05.930 --> 00:09:07.390
undetected.

00:09:07.390 --> 00:09:11.509
If you read the newspaper coverage at the
time, you’d be forgiven for thinking this

00:09:11.509 --> 00:09:16.939
was some kind of elaborate heist pulled off
by sophisticated international art thieves.

00:09:16.939 --> 00:09:23.869
But as we’ve seen, security at some of Paris’
most famous landmarks isn’t always quite

00:09:23.869 --> 00:09:30.259
what you’d expect, and in reality, the theft
was pulled off by some bloke who ambled up

00:09:30.259 --> 00:09:36.209
to the Museum in the dead of night, broke
one padlock and smashed a single window.

00:09:36.209 --> 00:09:41.541
That was literally all it took to grant the
guy access to hundreds of millions of Euro

00:09:41.541 --> 00:09:43.910
worth of paintings.

00:09:43.910 --> 00:09:47.690
The robbery was nothing to do with Les UX
- as I’ve said, they’re in the business

00:09:47.690 --> 00:09:52.959
of preserving heritage, not pinching it - but
they have been doing their bit to try and

00:09:52.959 --> 00:09:59.110
ensure similar robberies never happen again
by keeping an eye on museum security across

00:09:59.110 --> 00:10:03.449
the city and stepping in when they find issues.

00:10:03.449 --> 00:10:07.649
Collectively they’re like French Batman,
but instead of causing millions of Euros of

00:10:07.649 --> 00:10:13.480
property damage and hospitalising just about
everyone in sight to stop a lone baddie, Les

00:10:13.480 --> 00:10:19.680
UX prefer to fix what’s broken and take
a more intelligent approach to vigilantism.

00:10:19.680 --> 00:10:24.300
One member carried out a detailed audit of
one of Paris’ leading museums over a series

00:10:24.300 --> 00:10:29.679
of weeks, finding multiple problems with the
level of security she observed.

00:10:29.679 --> 00:10:35.220
She wrote a full report on the various issues,
explaining the myriad ways she could have

00:10:35.220 --> 00:10:40.860
broken into the museum and made off with some
swag had she chosen to do so.

00:10:40.860 --> 00:10:46.930
When she’d finished, presumably both to
save on postage and to ably demonstrate her

00:10:46.930 --> 00:10:52.910
point, she infiltrated the office of the museum’s
head of security in the middle of the night

00:10:52.910 --> 00:10:55.300
and left the report on his desk.

00:10:55.300 --> 00:11:00.689
You’d think the man would have been grateful
for the help when he came in next morning,

00:11:00.689 --> 00:11:03.769
if perhaps a little embarrassed by where it
came from.

00:11:03.769 --> 00:11:05.190
But no.

00:11:05.190 --> 00:11:06.500
He wasn’t grateful.

00:11:06.500 --> 00:11:07.699
He was angry.

00:11:07.699 --> 00:11:12.329
So angry, in fact, he went straight to the
police to press charges.

00:11:12.329 --> 00:11:14.889
Talk about looking a gift horse in the mouth.

00:11:14.889 --> 00:11:18.420
The police declined to pursue the matter.

00:11:18.420 --> 00:11:23.319
Without a doubt Les UX’s crowning achievement
- or at least the crowning achievement among

00:11:23.319 --> 00:11:29.959
the ones we actually know about - took place
back where it all started - at the Panthéon

00:11:29.959 --> 00:11:35.559
- which, as it turns out, had become something
of a second home to the members of Les UX

00:11:35.559 --> 00:11:37.149
over the years.

00:11:37.149 --> 00:11:43.110
Since that first night, the group had been
back many, many times - and not just to wander

00:11:43.110 --> 00:11:47.350
around admiring the nineteenth century architectural
flourishes.

00:11:47.350 --> 00:11:52.540
As crazy as it sounds, not content simply
with infiltrating one of the city’s most

00:11:52.540 --> 00:12:00.739
famous landmarks, Les UX members had for years
been using the cavernous interior as their

00:12:00.739 --> 00:12:07.970
own private after-hours exhibition centre,
hosting art installations, staging plays,

00:12:07.970 --> 00:12:14.060
and filming screenings there in the dead of
night, then clearing everything away and disappearing

00:12:14.060 --> 00:12:18.519
without a trace before the tourists turned
up the next day.

00:12:18.519 --> 00:12:23.189
If you find that hard to believe you don’t
have to take my word for it, because Les UX

00:12:23.189 --> 00:12:28.389
kindly went to the trouble of producing a
short film of their exploits called ‘Panthéon,

00:12:28.389 --> 00:12:34.970
User Guide’, in which you can watch a Les
UX member in an Untergunther t-shirt enjoying

00:12:34.970 --> 00:12:40.300
a pleasant evening rollerblading around a
deserted Pantheon before other team members

00:12:40.300 --> 00:12:46.379
arrive to rig up a huge projector for a private
screening of American Psycho - all without

00:12:46.379 --> 00:12:50.689
staff at the mausoleum ever being any the
wiser.

00:12:50.689 --> 00:12:56.269
It was probably during one of these events
in November of 2005 that someone decided to

00:12:56.269 --> 00:13:02.489
go and take a look at one of the Panthéon’s
greatest treasures, an antique Wagner clock,

00:13:02.489 --> 00:13:07.699
which was built in 1850 to preside over an
prominent entrance within the building, but

00:13:07.699 --> 00:13:10.959
had been allowed to fall into disrepair.

00:13:10.959 --> 00:13:16.699
One of Les UX’s members happened to be a
qualified watchmaker for renowned French horological

00:13:16.699 --> 00:13:23.360
house Breguet, and on taking a look at the
piece he discovered it wasn’t simply old

00:13:23.360 --> 00:13:29.149
and dirty - someone had deliberately sabotaged
the movement, presumably to save them the

00:13:29.149 --> 00:13:31.730
trouble of winding it every day.

00:13:31.730 --> 00:13:36.579
If the Pantheon administration weren’t going
to look after such a precious piece under

00:13:36.579 --> 00:13:43.589
their care, it was time for the Untergunther,
Les UX’s specialist restoration unit, to

00:13:43.589 --> 00:13:44.799
step in.

00:13:44.799 --> 00:13:49.310
But this was no simple spruce-up of a forgotten
crypt beneath the city.

00:13:49.310 --> 00:13:54.430
The Wagner clock was an important artefact
in one of Paris’ most popular buildings,

00:13:54.430 --> 00:13:57.910
visited by hundreds of thousands of tourists
each year.

00:13:57.910 --> 00:14:04.279
The clock’s mechanism was large and complicated,
and it would need to be taken apart and thoroughly

00:14:04.279 --> 00:14:08.029
cleaned, with the damaged parts repaired or
replaced.

00:14:08.029 --> 00:14:13.239
And seeing as the work would have to be carried
out in secret during the night, they were

00:14:13.239 --> 00:14:18.499
going to need months to complete the job,
not to mention plenty of equipment - more

00:14:18.499 --> 00:14:21.420
than they could simply bring in and out every
night.

00:14:21.420 --> 00:14:26.269
Put all of these things together and it should
have been an impossible job.

00:14:26.269 --> 00:14:29.970
But the Untergunther were more than a match
for it.

00:14:29.970 --> 00:14:34.820
To kick things off, they built themselves
a fully functioning secret workshop in a cavity

00:14:34.820 --> 00:14:41.249
high up in the Panthéon, hidden beneath the
great dome, which they kitted out with armchairs,

00:14:41.249 --> 00:14:43.879
bookshelves and a small bar.

00:14:43.879 --> 00:14:51.509
And because they love a silly name, they dubbed
it the Unter and Gunther Winter Kneipe - German

00:14:51.509 --> 00:14:54.249
for ‘the Unter and Gunther Winter boozer’.

00:14:54.249 --> 00:14:58.590
They even set up a small vegetable garden
on a terrace overlooking the city, because

00:14:58.590 --> 00:15:04.610
getting your 5-a-day shouldn’t be compromised
even if you are busy covertly restoring an

00:15:04.610 --> 00:15:08.439
ageing artefact right under the nose of the
French government.

00:15:08.439 --> 00:15:12.730
The restoration took almost a year all told,
with the clock painstakingly restored to its

00:15:12.730 --> 00:15:14.360
former glory.

00:15:14.360 --> 00:15:20.509
Typically, Les UX act in complete secrecy
- we have no way of knowing how many such

00:15:20.509 --> 00:15:23.559
restorations they’ve made across the city.

00:15:23.559 --> 00:15:25.989
But this time was different.

00:15:25.989 --> 00:15:30.190
After all, in order for their hard work to
be worthwhile, somebody was going to have

00:15:30.190 --> 00:15:32.559
to keep the Wagner clock wound.

00:15:32.559 --> 00:15:40.100
So, for once breaking protocol, Les UX surfaced,
arranging a meeting with the administrator

00:15:40.100 --> 00:15:42.350
of the Panthéon to tell him the good news.

00:15:42.350 --> 00:15:47.689
You’re probably thinking the man was absolutely
delighted to hear an important part of the

00:15:47.689 --> 00:15:53.199
Panthéon’s history had been repaired completely
free of charge by a horological expert.

00:15:53.199 --> 00:16:00.119
And rumour has it he was - for all of about
fifteen seconds, at which point he didn’t

00:16:00.119 --> 00:16:05.149
so much look his gift horse in the mouth but
attempted to get it sent straight to the glue

00:16:05.149 --> 00:16:06.459
factory.

00:16:06.459 --> 00:16:10.209
Apparently coming to the conclusion the whole
thing was going to make him look incompetent

00:16:10.209 --> 00:16:16.109
– after all, not one member of his staff
reported seeing anything out of the ordinary

00:16:16.109 --> 00:16:21.290
during the entire year it had taken for the
team to restore the clock - the man promptly

00:16:21.290 --> 00:16:27.290
initiated legal action against those involved,
with prosecutors seeking up to a year in jail

00:16:27.290 --> 00:16:35.109
on top of almost €50,000 in damages, despite
the fact Les UX had invested some €4000

00:16:35.109 --> 00:16:37.639
of their own money on the restoration.

00:16:37.639 --> 00:16:44.029
The Panthéon even hired another clockmaker
to undo the Untergunther’s repairs, although

00:16:44.029 --> 00:16:49.449
he refused to actively damage the mechanism,
deactivating it instead.

00:16:49.449 --> 00:16:55.459
Unfortunately for the pantheon administration,
it seems there are no laws in France against

00:16:55.459 --> 00:17:00.619
secretly repairing clocks in the middle of
the night, so instead they went after Les

00:17:00.619 --> 00:17:03.309
UX for something else entirely.

00:17:03.309 --> 00:17:08.750
Inside a year, four members of the Untergunther
found themselves in the Paris court of justice

00:17:08.750 --> 00:17:16.089
not on charges of breaking and entering, or
of repairing a priceless clock, but for apparently

00:17:16.089 --> 00:17:19.269
breaking a lock somewhere on the Panthéon
grounds.

00:17:19.269 --> 00:17:24.000
It was a ridiculous case in many, many ways,
not least because there was absolutely no

00:17:24.000 --> 00:17:28.549
proof Untergunther members had broken the
lock in the first place.

00:17:28.549 --> 00:17:33.549
But thankfully, the French legal system came
through with flying colours on this one.

00:17:33.549 --> 00:17:39.210
After hearing the trial, the judge deliberated
for only 20 minutes before revealing his verdict

00:17:39.210 --> 00:17:45.500
- not guilty, with one of the prosecutors
quoted as calling the whole case ‘stupid’.

00:17:45.500 --> 00:17:51.620
My grasp of French law is pretty basic, but
I think the judge came to his decision based

00:17:51.620 --> 00:17:57.049
on reasoning along the lines of ‘we don’t
guillotine the good guys, Mon Amie.’

00:17:57.049 --> 00:17:59.230
Moron amie, more like.

00:17:59.230 --> 00:18:04.940
In the kind of two finger salute they’re
now famous for, Les UX infiltrated the Panthéon

00:18:04.940 --> 00:18:10.980
once more in the wake of the trial, reactivating
the Wagner clock so that it would chime for

00:18:10.980 --> 00:18:16.730
a few days over the Christmas period, before
returning once again to remove the component

00:18:16.730 --> 00:18:22.309
they’d repaired, just in case the current
administration hired anyone else to break

00:18:22.309 --> 00:18:23.309
it.

00:18:23.309 --> 00:18:28.730
What’s so intriguing about Les UX is that
everything we know about them - the remarkable

00:18:28.730 --> 00:18:35.440
restoration of the Pantheon clock, the mysterious
security reports designed to protect important

00:18:35.440 --> 00:18:42.600
art galleries, the infiltration and the subterranean
events - these things just scratch the surface

00:18:42.600 --> 00:18:45.450
of their clandestine activities beneath Paris.

00:18:45.450 --> 00:18:51.740
They are, after all, an underground organisation,
and they don’t publicise almost anything

00:18:51.740 --> 00:18:52.929
they do.

00:18:52.929 --> 00:18:57.130
What we do know about them has often come
out inadvertently when someone they’ve tried

00:18:57.130 --> 00:19:02.490
to help has reacted with about as much grace
as Bambi after fifteen pints of Newcastle

00:19:02.490 --> 00:19:03.690
Brown Ale.

00:19:03.690 --> 00:19:09.000
On the rare occasions Les UX members have
spoken to the press, they’ve always hinted

00:19:09.000 --> 00:19:14.710
at other, equally ambitious projects they’re
working on that never make it into the public

00:19:14.710 --> 00:19:15.710
domain.

00:19:15.710 --> 00:19:19.891
But if you think all of this sounds like kind
of a good time, I’m afraid there’s no

00:19:19.891 --> 00:19:22.390
easy way of joining Les UX.

00:19:22.390 --> 00:19:29.120
Just to add a little more to their mystique,
becoming a member is by invitation only, and

00:19:29.120 --> 00:19:35.470
only those who are already involved in ‘Les
UX-like activities’ will ever receive an

00:19:35.470 --> 00:19:36.470
invite.

00:19:36.470 --> 00:19:41.710
So yeah, if you want to join, you basically
have to go to Paris, get yourself underground

00:19:41.710 --> 00:19:47.080
sharpish, and start causing all sorts of mischief
until some suave looking stranger taps you

00:19:47.080 --> 00:19:51.169
on the shoulder in a coffee shop and teaches
you the secret handshake.

00:19:51.169 --> 00:19:53.590
Well, I guess that’s my summer holiday sorted.

00:19:53.590 --> 00:20:05.200
Thanks for Watching.

